// A cyclic `JsonValue` and the guard that refuses it.
//
// Nova arrays are heap references, so three lines close a loop. Before this
// increment the recursive `stringify` met it with a stack overflow: fatal,
// under a second, and carrying no message -- MEASURED, by running this exact
// program against the still-recursive form before this guard existed. The
// iterative form has no such natural floor either: unguarded, the work list
// grows one net item per pop with nothing to stop it. What that ends in is
// REASONING, not something this fixture has measured -- an unguarded run of
// this exact program was killed after 20 seconds still running, not run to
// completion, so "an allocator abort that prints neither a `nova: panic:`
// prefix nor a location" is the expected shape, not an observed one. The
// guard turns the cyclic case back into an immediate, named failure, which
// is better than either outcome, observed or not.
//
// The guard is on NESTING DEPTH and not on work-list length, deliberately: a
// cycle grows depth without bound while a wide, shallow document grows only
// length, and a length bound would refuse legitimate input.
//
// THIS FIXTURE IS A WEAK MUTATION DETECTOR FOR THE GUARD, ON PURPOSE LEFT
// THAT WAY. A cyclic value is genuinely non-terminating once the guard is
// gone, so deleting the guard turns this test into a hang rather than a
// fast, named failure -- there is no assertion about a cyclic input that can
// fail quickly, because the input itself never finishes. This fixture still
// pins the cycle case, which is what the guard exists for; the deleted-guard
// mutation is instead caught promptly by the sibling fixture
// `json_render_guard.nova`, whose input is acyclic and finite either way.
fn main() {
    let mut a: [JsonValue] = [Null]
    let v = Array(a)
    a[0] = v
    println("about to render a cyclic value")
    println("${stringify(v).len()}")
}
